      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244048/assets/aa4fdc44e56d2e6b55bc649f97dace1d9a270f8212f3a28da962059f9f1a6ad7.png)\n\nFind the minimum value of the parabola $y=(x-5)^{2}-5$, sketched above.",
      "worked_solution": "The minimum value of the parabola is the $y$-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola. \n\nRemember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe given parabola\n\n$$\ny=(x-5)^{2}-5\n$$\n\nis written in vertex form with $h=5$ and $k=-5.$ Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at $(5,-5),$ and its minimum value is $-5.$",
